  "prediction": "**Leopard Attacks and Kills Toddler in Queen Elizabeth National Park, Uganda**\n\nA devastating incident has occurred in Queen Elizabeth National Park, Uganda, where a leopard attacked and killed a 2-year-old toddler. The child, Elisha Nabugyere, was left in the care of a nanny at the staff quarters of Mweya Safari Lodge when the leopard snatched him on Friday night. The nanny heard the child scream for help, but it was too late, and the leopard had vanished with the toddler into the bush.\n\nAccording to reports, the child's mother, a game ranger at the park, was working at the time of the incident. The father, SSP Francis Manana Nabugyere, an officer at the police senior Command and staff college Bwebajja, expressed his shock and grief over the loss of his son. He has called for heightened security measures to protect staff and children at the park.\n\nThe Uganda Wildlife Authority (UWA) has launched a hunt to capture the leopard, which is considered a threat to human life after consuming human flesh. UWA communications manager Bashir Hangi stated that efforts are underway to track down the leopard and evaluate options such as relocating it to a different area.\n\nThe incident has sparked outrage and concern among locals and tourists, with many calling for improved security measures at the park. The UWA has promised to compensate the family for their loss, but the exact details of the compensation package are yet to be disclosed.\n\nThis tragic incident is not an isolated one, as it comes on the heels of several other unfortunate events at the park in recent weeks. A pride of 11 lions died on April 11, a French tourist died on April 14, and another lion died at the end of April. The killing of the toddler has raised questions about the safety and security of the park, and the UWA has vowed to take steps to prevent such incidents in the future.\n\nThe incident has also highlighted the importance of protecting wildlife and their habitats, while also ensuring the safety of humans who live and work in close proximity to these animals. As the UWA continues its efforts to capture the leopard and prevent future attacks, the community is left to mourn the loss of a young life and reflect on the importance of coexisting with wildlife.",
